 { Henrique Mecking - Sandro Mareco, Campinas 2011 Learning objective: This game illustrates the powerful d4-d5 break and the strong attack White obtains from it. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. Nf3 c5 7. O-O cxd4 8. exd4 dxc4 9. Bxc4 b6 10. Qb3 Bxc3 11. bxc3 { We have reached the position of interest. This is a nice version of the hanging pawns for White, as he has the pair of bishops in an open position. } Bb7 ( { PH: The move } 11... Bb7 { is the cause of Black's problems. Instead, } Nc6 $1 { , immediately threatening ...Na5, disrupts White's setup more. For example } 12. Be2 Bb7 { followed by ...Na5 and ...Rc8 would put more pressure on White. } ) 
12. Ba3 Re8 13. Bb5 Bc6 14. Be2 { A typical manoeuvre. White wants to play Bc4-e2 to push c3-c4. By playing Bc4-b5 first, White causes Black's bishop to become misplaced on c6, where it will be vulnerable. Sooner or later Black will have to waste a tempo with ...Bc6-b7. } Nbd7 15. c4 Qc7 16. Rfe1 Rad8 ( { The break } 16... e5 $6 { hoping to weaken the hanging pawns, does not work well because White is ready to push d4-d5; his pieces are very active and the c4-pawn is a difficult target to attack. } 17. d5 Bb7 18. h3 Nc5 19. Qe3 { White has a pleasant position, and the game could continue with: } Nfe4 ( { Or } 19... Nfd7 20. Rad1 Rac8 21. Nh4 g6 22. Bg4 $1 { ± With strong pressure. } f5 $2 { fails due to } 23. Bxf5 $1 gxf5 24. Nxf5 { with a deadly attack. } ) 
20. Nd2 Nxd2 21. Qxd2 Qd6 22. Bd1 Rac8 23. Re3 Re7 24. Bc2 f6 25. Rae1 $16 { White can continue to progress with h4-h5 and Rg3. } ) 
 ( { PH: Perhaps better was } 16... Bxf3 $5 17. Bxf3 Rac8 18. Be2 e5 { , trying to set up a blockade on the dark squares. A sample continuation is } 19. d5 Nc5 20. Qe3 Nfe4 21. Bb2 f6 22. a4 a5 23. h4 Nd6 24. h5 h6 25. Bf1 { White is the one pressing, but Black's position seems very defendable. } ) 
17. Bb2 Nf8 $6 { There is no reason to give away the centre so easily. } ( { Better was } 17... Ng4 { provoking White to weaken his long diagonal with g2-g3. A: White is not ready for the break } 18. d5 $6 { due to: } Bb7 19. h3 Ngf6 20. dxe6 Rxe6 $11 ( { B: } 17... Nf8 17. g3 Ngf6 19. Qe3 $14 ) 
 ( { C: } 17... Nf8 17. h3 Bxf3 19. Bxf3 Qh2 20. Kf1 Ngf6 { when chances are approximately balanced. For example: } 21. Rad1 Qh1 22. Ke2 Qh2 23. Qe3 $6 ( 22... Nf8 22. Kf1 $11 ) 
Qc7 $1 24. Rc1 e5 $1 { ⩱ and Black gains the initiative. } ) 
 ) 
