27. dxe6 fxe6 ( 27... Rd2 28. Re2 Rxe2 29. Qxe2 fxe6 30. cxb5 { and White wins. } ) 
28. Rad1 $18 { A simple yet beautiful move which illustrates White's overwhelming initiative once d4-d5 is achieved. Black has no hope of dealing with the multiple threats, the most direct being Rxd7 followed by Qxh4. } ( { The imprecise } 28. Rxe6 $2 { is met by } Rd2 $1 29. Re2 Rxe2 30. Qxe2 Qg5 { threatening ...Bxf3. } 31. Rf1 bxc4 $16 { when Black is holding the game for longer. } ) 
Rxd1 29. Rxd1 Qg5 30. Qg3 $1 { Nice move! It forces Black to choose between a lost endgame and allowing a mating attack. } Qe7 ( { Or } 30... Qxg3 31. hxg3 Ng6 32. cxb5 { winning due to the bishop pair, the initiative and the extra pawn. } ) 
31. Rd7 $1 ( { Black resigned, in view of } 31. Rd7 Qxd7 32. Qxh4 { and there is no satisfactory way to prevent Qxh7#. Final remarks 1. It was not possible for Black just to break the hanging pawns with 16... e5. White's good piece placement rendered this move ineffective. 2. White's pair of bishops played a central role in many positions. They are very helpful in an open position such as with hanging pawns. Black seemingly underestimated the decisive d4-d5 break. It was necessary to prevent it, though passive defence would have been unpleasant as well. } ) 

