 { Anton Korobov - Bartlomiej Macieja, Warsaw 2010 Learning objective: The hanging pawns structure can be modified after pieces are traded on e5 and White captures with dxe5. This game is an example of how White's attack is conducted in such situations. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 c5 6. Nf3 d5 7. O-O cxd4 8. exd4 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Nbd7 10. Bd3 b6 11. a3 Bxc3 12. bxc3 Bb7 ( { PH: Here } 12... Qc7 { is sharper, asking White what he is going to do with c3. White could consider } 13. Bb2 { which is a good alternative square to g5 for the bishop in these hanging pawn structures. For example } Bb7 14. c4 Qf4 $5 { Black is trying to make use of the dark-squared bishop's absence on this diagonal. } 15. a4 Bxf3 16. Qxf3 Qxf3 17. gxf3 $14 { in this endgame the bishop pair is probably more important than White's somewhat weaker pawn structure. } ) 
13. Re1 Re8 14. Bg5 ( { PH: } 14. c4 $5 Qc7 15. Ne5 $1 { , keeping the c1-bishop flexible, was stronger. } ) 
Qc7 15. c4 Rac8 16. Ne5 $14 { After a standard opening, we have reached the position of interest. White's pieces are arranged to create a kingside attack and Black must react carefully. } ( { PH: The computer says } 16. a4 $5 { is even stronger, intending to soften up the b6-pawn while exchanging off White's own isolated pawn on the a-file. It's not clear how Black should respond; } e5 { would be typical, but then } 17. Bf5 $1 { , intending Bxf6 when Black has to retake with the g-pawn is threatened. } Rb8 18. d5 $1 Qxc4 19. d6 { and the d-pawn is very dangerous and provides more than enough compensation for the pawn. } ) 
Nxe5 ( { After } 16... h6 17. Nxd7 $5 ( { Or } 16... Nxe5 16. Bf4 Nxe5 18. Bxe5 Qc6 19. f3 $14 ) 
Nxd7 18. Bh4 { White's bishop pair gives him good play, say after } Qf4 19. Bg3 Qf6 ( 19... Qxd4 $4 20. Bh7 $18 ) 
20. a4 $14 { followed by a4-a5 to weaken Black's queenside. } ) 
17. dxe5 $6 { This is a typical structure transformation with hanging pawns. The c4 pawn is weakened, while the kingside attack gains strength. A key factor is who gains the initiative after this change. } ( { Better was } 17. Rxe5 Nd7 18. Re3 { with good attacking prospects, for example } h6 19. Bh4 Nf8 20. Rg3 $14 { threatening Bf6. PH: After } 17. Rxe5 { Black should play } Qc6 $1 { . If White plays } 18. f3 { then there is no rook lift anymore, while } 18. Bf1 { is met by the annoying } Ne4 $1 19. Be3 Nd6 { with counterplay against c4, while ...Nf5 is an idea as well. Black has a good position. } ) 
Nd7 $2 { This seemingly natural move is a near-decisive mistake, as it allows White to gain the initiative. } ( { The intermediate } 17... Qc6 $1 { was absolutely necessary: A. } 18. f3 $2 { is severely punished by } Ng4 $1 { and after } 19. Qe2 ( { • Or } 18... Nd7 18. Bh4 Qc5 20. Kh1 Nxe5 $19 ) 
 ( { • } 18... Nd7 18. fxg4 $4 Qxg2 ) 
Qc5 20. Be3 Nxe3 21. Qxe3 Ba6 $17 { the c4-pawn and the game are probably lost. } ( { B. } 17... Nd7 17. exf6 $4 Qxg2 ) 
 ( { C. } 17... Nd7 17. Bf1 Ne4 19. Bh4 $13 { Chances are balanced, as Black controls key central squares while White retains some attacking chances. } ) 
 ) 
18. Qh5 $16 { Black is already in deep trouble and extreme precision is needed to stay in the game. } Nf8 $6 { Black does not want to weaken the kingside by moving pawns, but this move only aggravates the situation as it gives White time to create further threats. } ( { Better was } 18... g6 { creating some weaknesses but at least blocking the bishop on d3; then after } 19. Qh6 { threatening to checkmate with Bf6: } Qc5 20. Re3 Qf8 21. Qh4 $16 { White has a dangerous attack. } ) 
19. Re3 { Threatening Bxh7 and Rh3. } Qc6 ( { In the event of } 19... Ng6 { Black's position becomes desperate after } 20. Rae1 $1 { threatening 21. Rh3. } h6 ( { Or } 20... Qc6 21. Be4 Qc7 22. Rh3 h6 23. Bxh6 gxh6 24. Bc2 $1 { +- with the deadly threats Qxh6 and Rg3. } ) 
21. Bxh6 gxh6 22. Qxh6 Rcd8 23. h4 $1 { +- Followed by h4-h5 winning. } ) 
20. Rg3 Rc7 $2 { Making things easier. } ( { More stubborn was: } 20... Ng6 21. Re1 { A prophylactic move defending the e5-pawn against the potential ...Qc5xe5. Now White's threat of h2-h4-h5 is practically winning. } Rc7 $1 { Aiming to create counterplay with ...Rd7xd3. } ( 21... Qc5 $2 { loses immediately to } 22. Rh3 $1 h6 23. Bxh6 gxh6 24. Bxg6 ) 
22. h4 Rd7 23. Qe2 Nxe5 ( { Or } 23... Ne7 24. Bxe7 Rdxe7 25. Qg4 g6 26. h5 $18 { followed by hxg6 and Bxg6. } ) 
24. Bxh7 ( 23... Rc7 23. Qxe5 $2 Rxd3 $1 { is equal since } 25. Rxd3 $4 { allows } Qxg2 ) 
Kxh7 25. Qh5 Kg8 26. Rxe5 { White is winning, as 27. Bf6 is coming. } ) 
21. Bxh7+ $18 { Checkmate cannot be avoided. } Nxh7 22. Bf6 Qxc4 ( 22... g6 23. Qxh7 $1 Kxh7 24. Rh3 Kg8 25. Rh8 ) 
23. Rxg7+ Kf8 24. Qh6 Rec8 25. Rg8+ { Final remarks Black essentially lost this game in one move by playing 17... Nd7. After the capture dxe5, gaining the initiative is necessary to fight for the advantage or even just to stay in the game. } 
