 { Sandro Mareco - Christian Toth, Osasco 2012 Learning objective: This game illustrates how the hanging pawns may be blocked and subsequently attacked. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Bxd2+ 5. Nxd2 d5 ( { More common is } 5... d6 { followed by ...e6-e5, especially since White's knight is on d2. } ) 
6. Bg2 O-O 7. Ngf3 b6 8. O-O Bb7 9. Rc1 Nbd7 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Ne5 c5 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. dxc5 { After a careless opening, Black is already in a difficult position. } bxc5 $6 { Black obtains a terrible version of the typical hanging pawn structure, due to a simple tactical problem. } ( { Better was: } 13... Nxc5 14. Nb3 Ne6 15. Qd2 Qf6 16. e3 $14 { with a pleasant version for White of an isolani position; compare the game Vitiugov - Bologan from the previous chapter. } ) 
14. Nb3 $1 { Black cannot maintain his hanging pawns in the ideal c5-d5 position. } ( { PH: } 14. Nb3 { is strong, but } 14. Nb1 { is at least just as good. Black can't answer } Nf6 { as it hangs c5, while after } Qg5 15. Nc3 { Black's center is also impossible to keep together. } ) 
c4 { This advance is undesirable because it loses control of the d4-square. Unfortunately, there was no choice. } ( { Of course not } 14... Rc8 $2 15. Bxd5 ) 
15. Nd4 $14 { White's advantage is evident. The chain d5-c4 is weak and does not contribute to the control of the centre. } Qa5 ( { An alternative was: } 15... Ne5 16. b3 Qd7 17. Nf3 $1 Nxf3 18. Bxf3 cxb3 19. Qxb3 $14 { Black has a difficult defence ahead due to the permanently weakened d5-pawn. } ) 
 ( { Or } 15... Nf6 16. b3 Ba6 17. Nf5 cxb3 18. axb3 Re8 19. Re1 $14 { when White has a great version of the isolani. } ) 
16. b3 $1 { Breaking the chain d5-c4. } Rab8 ( { The a2-pawn is untouchable with } 16... Qxa2 $2 { due to } 17. Ra1 $1 Qb2 18. bxc4 { Threatening Rb1. } Nc5 ( { Or } 18... dxc4 $2 19. Rb1 ) 
19. cxd5 { and the central pawns decide the game. } ) 
17. Nf5 { White begins to turn his positional dominance into an attack. An immediate threat is Ne7xd5. } Nf6 18. Rc2 Rfd8 ( { Better was: } 18... cxb3 19. axb3 Qb6 ( { There is no time to pressure the b3-pawn with } 19... Ba8 { due to } 20. Qc1 $1 Rxb3 21. Rc8 $1 ( { Or } 20... Rfd8 20. Nxg7 $5 { +- } ) 
Qb4 22. Qg5 g6 23. Qxf6 gxf5 ( 23... Rxc8 $4 24. Qg7 ) 
24. Qg5 Kh8 25. Qxf5 $18 { when the exposure of Black's king makes his position desperate. } ) 
20. Qd4 $1 { 20. Qa1!?. } Qxd4 ( { Black runs into fatal problems after } 20... Qxb3 $2 21. Rb2 Qc4 22. Rfb1 { as the bishop is lost. } ) 
21. Nxd4 { With a pleasant long-term advantage. } ) 
19. Qc1 $16 { White's forces are well coordinated, and now he is ready to embark on a kingside attack. The most immediate threats are Qg5 and Nxg7. } h6 ( { An alternative was } 19... cxb3 20. axb3 Qb6 21. Qg5 Ne8 22. Rfc1 $16 { when White dominates. Black is one mistake away from losing, for example } Qxb3 $2 23. Nh6 Kf8 24. Rc7 $1 Nxc7 25. Rxc7 { when the checkmate threat is unstoppable: } Qa3 26. Rxf7 Ke8 27. Qe5 Qe7 28. Qxe7 ) 
20. Qf4 { Threatening Nxh6+. } cxb3 21. axb3 Qb6 22. Rfc1 d4 $2 { Making the attack easier. } ( 22... Qxb3 { does not help: } 23. Nxh6 $1 gxh6 24. Qxf6 $18 { White has a much better structure and a much safer king. } ) 
 ( { The best defence was } 22... Ne8 23. Rc5 Qe6 24. Nd4 Qf6 25. Qxf6 Nxf6 26. Rb5 $16 { though White has great chances to win this endgame. } ) 
23. Bxb7 Rxb7 ( 23... Qxb7 24. Nxh6 gxh6 25. Qxf6 Qxb3 26. Qxh6 { and wins. } ) 
24. Rc6 Qxb3 25. Rxf6 $1 gxf6 26. Qxh6 { Black resigned in view of 27. Qg7#. Final remarks 1. Black's biggest mistake was accepting a hanging pawn structure even though his pawns would be blocked from the start. 2. This game is a fine example of how a good strategic concept can be realized tactically. } 
 1-0

