 { Bartlomiej Macieja - Holden Hernandez, Mexico City 2012 Learning objectives: 1. Black's imprecise opening play allows the hanging pawns to be blocked ideally. 2. We then learn how White may use this fact to disrupt the centre with the standard e3-e4 break, gaining a big advantage. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 a5 5. g3 d5 6. Qc2 c5 7. cxd5 cxd4 8. Bg2 O-O 9. Nxd4 Qb6 10. e3 exd5 11. O-O Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. Rc1 { I was surprised to find eight games at the GM-level with this position. Black experiences serious difficulties due to his inability to achieve the ...c6-c5 advance in time. } Bxd2 $6 { Accelerating White's plans. The first objective will be to control c5 with Nb3-c5. } ( { More accurate is } 13... Bd7 { but it does not quite solve Black's problems after: } 14. Bc3 Be7 ( { Or } 14... Bxc3 15. Nxc3 Rfb8 16. Na4 Qa7 17. b3 $14 { and White has a lasting advantage due to the blockade on c5. } ) 
15. Bd4 Qb7 16. Nc3 Rfb8 ( 16... c5 $2 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Nxd5 $18 ) 
17. Na4 $16 { This is a position to remember. The blockade Bd4, Nc5 turns the c6/d5 pawns into serious weaknesses. Black has no chance to carry out any of the typical plans in a hanging pawns position. } ) 
 ( { PH: Now in 2024 we have more than a 100 Grandmaster games in this position, and the conclusion is that Black is absolutely fine! But he should probably play } 13... Rb8 { . Now if } 14. Qxc6 { Black will just exchange on d2 and then take the b2-pawn, so perhaps } 14. Nc3 { , but here Black has the strong } h5 $1 { as White's kingside is a bit lacking defenders. For example } 15. Na4 Qd8 { and Black can continue with ...h4 and maneuver the queen to g5. } ) 
14. Nxd2 Bd7 15. Nb3 Rfb8 16. Nc5 Be8 ( { The capture } 16... Qxb2 { does not solve the problems after } 17. Nxd7 Nxd7 ( 17... Qxc2 $4 18. Nxf6 $16 ) 
18. Qxc6 Nf6 19. Bxd5 Nxd5 20. Qxd5 $16 { when White has good chances of winning this endgame. } ) 
17. b3 Qa7 { White has blocked the central pawns and his pieces dominate the board. Hence, it is a good moment for the central break: } 18. e4 $1 { Permanently damaging the hanging pawns. } h6 $6 { This allows White to rearrange his pieces. } ( { The best defence was } 18... Rb5 $14 { preventing the transfer Rc1-e1 followed by Ra1-c1. } ) 
19. Re1 $1 dxe4 20. Bxe4 ( { PH: } 20. Nxe4 $16 { is just a stable advantage for White. After } 20. Bxe4 $6 { Black could have taken } Nxe4 21. Rxe4 f6 { , when the weakened light squares on White's kingside give Black some attacking chances. } ) 
Rb5 21. Rac1 Nxe4 22. Rxe4 $16 { With a big positional advantage which soon turns into a kingside attack. } f6 23. Qc3 Bf7 24. Qe3 Qb8 25. Re7 Qf8 $6 ( { Better was } 25... Qd8 { though White has several attractive options such as } 26. Rxf7 $5 Kxf7 27. Qe6 Kg6 28. Qe4 Kf7 29. Qxc6 Rb6 30. Ne6 Rxc6 31. Nxd8 Rxd8 32. Rxc6 $16 { with excellent winning chances. } ) 
 { Now White could decide the game immediately with: } 26. a4 $1 ( { Instead, White played } 26. Re1 $2 Rbb8 27. Nd7 Qxe7 $1 28. Qxe7 Re8 29. Qxe8 Bxe8 30. Nc5 $14 { and eventually won the endgame. } ) 
Rb4 ( 26... Rbb8 { also loses to } 27. Nd7 ) 
27. Nd7 Qd8 28. Rd1 $1 { Threatening Nxf6+ followed by Rxd8. The key to 26. a4! is that now Black does not have 28...Rd5 available. } Bd5 ( 28... Qc8 { loses to } 29. Nb6 Rxb6 30. Qxb6 ) 
29. Rxd5 $1 cxd5 30. Nxf6+ $1 Kh8 ( 30... Kf8 31. Qe6 Qxe7 32. Qg8 ( 31... gxf6 32. Qf7 ) 
 ) 
31. Qxh6+ gxh6 32. Rh7# { Final remarks 1. Black's opening needs improvement. The hanging pawns will always be inferior if they can readily be blocked as in this game. 2. The break e3-e4 not only forced Black to accept a weak and isolated c6-pawn, but also opened lines for White's attack. } 
